Output: <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>
</code></pre><h4 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>
<p>The input will contain at least one interval.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>For every index <code>i</code> in the list, <code>intervals[i].start &lt=
; intervals[i].end</code>.</p>
</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>Think about what it means when x intervals overlap at a certain point in=
 time. This means at this point, there are x =E2=80=9C<strong>active</stron=
g>=E2=80=9D intervals, where an interval is active if it has started but no=
t ended.</p>
<p>In the example below, we see three active intervals at time 5 (intervals=
 that started at or before this time, and haven=E2=80=99t yet ended):</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a horizontal timeline ranging from 1 to 8, =
depicting four horizontal line segments representing intervals.  Each segme=
nt starts with a filled circle and ends with an unfilled circle, indicating=
 a start and end point respectively. Three of these segments overlap within=
 a region highlighted by a dashed orange rectangle around the 5 mark on the=
 timeline. A curved orange arrow points from this overlapping region to a g=
rey box labeled '3 active intervals,' further specifying that these three i=
ntervals 'have started' and 'haven't yet ended.' The fourth segment lies en=
tirely to the left of the orange dashed rectangle, indicating it has alread=
y ended.  The lengths of the segments vary, representing different duration=
s of the intervals." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"540" height=3D"128" decoding=
=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/cod=
ing-patterns/intervals/largest-overlap-of-intervals/image-09-03-1-NNENYBVW.=
svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div></figure>
<p>To find the number of active intervals at any point in time, we need to =
identify when an interval has started and when an interval has ended. The s=
tart point of an interval indicates the start of a new active interval, whe=
reas an end point represents an active interval finishing. This suggests an=
 approach which looks at start and end points individually could be useful.=
 Let=E2=80=99s explore this idea further.</p>
<p><strong>Processing start points and end points</strong><br>
Let=E2=80=99s step through each point in the array of intervals in chronolo=
gical order, and see if we can determine the number of active intervals at =
each point.</p>
<hr>
<p>The first point is a start point, indicating the start of the first acti=
ve interval. So, let=E2=80=99s increment our counter:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: left;"=
><img alt=3D"Image represents a diagram illustrating a coding pattern, poss=
ibly related to interval management or event tracking.  The diagram feature=
