XE7U5LK.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div></figure>
<hr>
<p>The next point is another start point. This is the start of the second a=
ctive interval, so let=E2=80=99s increment our counter again. Now, the numb=
er of active intervals is 2, which correctly corresponds with the number of=
 overlapping intervals at this point:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: left;"=
><img alt=3D"Image represents a visualization of an algorithm, possibly rel=
ated to interval scheduling or similar.  The diagram shows a number line sp=
anning from 1 to 8, with several horizontal line segments representing inte=
rvals. A light-blue circle at point 2 on the number line indicates a starti=
ng point, marked by a downward-pointing arrow originating from a square box=
 labeled 'i'.  Three intervals are depicted: one extending from 1 to 3, ano=
ther from 4 to 7, and a shorter one from 6 to 7.  A separate, dashed-line b=
ox to the right displays a code snippet: 'start point =E2=86=92 active_inte=
rvals +=3D 1 =3D 2', indicating that upon reaching the start point (2), the=
 variable `active_intervals` is incremented by 1, resulting in a value of 2=
. This suggests the algorithm counts or tracks active intervals based on th=
e starting point and the overlapping intervals on the number line." loading=
=3D"lazy" width=3D"624" height=3D"81" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" sr=
